= Ashwagandha

_Ayurvedic status: fully classical — aśvagandhā is one of the leading rasāyana and vājīkaraṇa drugs of the saṃhitās, and the canonical Vāta-Kapha rejuvenative of the South Asian tradition. Chinese: not classical — no traditional Chinese entry; modern adoption only. Greco-Roman: not classical — Withania does not appear in Greco-Roman pharmacy; modern Western use is a 20th-century import from Ayurveda._

== Nomenclature & Etymology

*Latin:* _Withania somnifera_ (L.) Dunal, family Solanaceae. The genus _Withania_ commemorates Henry Witham (1779–1844), English paleobotanist; the species was published by Linnaeus as _Physalis somnifera_ and transferred to _Withania_ by Dunal. _somnifera_ = "sleep-bringing" (_somnus_ "sleep" + _ferre_ "to bear") — the Latin epithet captures one of the drug's classical actions, _nidrā-janana_ (sleep-promoting), and is one of the rare instances where a Linnaean epithet aligns precisely with a tradition-internal claim. The drug is the *root* (_Withaniae radix_); the *leaf* (_Withaniae folium_) and *fruit* are used in some preparations.#footnote[Etymology: Lewis & Short, _A Latin Dictionary_ (s.v. _somnus_, _ferre_); standard botanical authorities for the genus dedication (e.g. Burkhardt, _Eponymisches Wörterbuch der Botanik_). Drug parts: Ayurvedic Pharmacopoeia of India, Part I, vol. 1, _Aśvagandhā_ monograph; root is the principal drug.]

*English:* _Ashwagandha_, direct transliteration of Sanskrit अश्वगन्धा (_aśvagandhā_). Common names: _winter cherry_ (from the fruit's red, persistent calyx — like a Chinese-lantern Physalis), _Indian ginseng_ (a popular but misleading parallel — Withania and Panax are unrelated and pharmacologically distinct; the name flatters by analogy but obscures both traditions). _Withania_ is also used as an English name for the genus in pharmacy.#footnote[_Oxford English Dictionary_ (s.v. "ashwagandha", "winter cherry"); the "Indian ginseng" usage is widespread in modern Western herbalism and is the standard popular misnomer — flagged as misleading.]

*Chinese:* No classical entry. Modern Chinese: 印度人參 (yìndù rénshēn, "Indian ginseng" — the popular calque of the Western misnomer); 睡茄 (shuìqié, "sleep eggplant" — calque of _somnifera_ + Solanaceae); the formal botanical name is 南非醉茄 (nánfēi zuìqié, "South-African drunken eggplant" — reflecting the wider African distribution of the species). None of these is a classical drug name; all are modern.#footnote[Modern Chinese terms: standard botanical / pharmaceutical vocabulary; cf. _Flora of China_, vol. 17 (Solanaceae). The 印度人參 / 睡茄 forms are modern transliteration / calque, not classical attribution.]

*Sanskrit:* अश्वगन्धा (_aśvagandhā_), "horse-smell" — from अश्व (_aśva_, "horse") + गन्ध (_gandha_, "smell/scent"). Two well-attested traditional readings of the name: (1) the fresh root smells (faintly) like horse urine — a literal claim, repeated in commentary; (2) the drug confers horse-like strength and sexual vigor — the metaphorical _vāji-_ ("steed-") reading. Both are classical; both are part of the tradition. Synonym set: वाजिगन्धा (_vājigandhā_, "steed-scented"), हयगन्धा (_hayagandhā_, the same with another horse-word), वराहकर्णी (_varāhakarṇī_, "boar-eared" — from the leaf shape), बलदा (_baladā_, "strength-giving"), वरदा (_varadā_, "boon-granting").#footnote[Monier-Williams, _A Sanskrit–English Dictionary_ (s.v. _aśvagandhā_, _vājigandhā_, _aśva_, _gandha_); synonym lists from Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Guḍūcyādi-varga_, and Dhanvantari Nighaṇṭu. The double-reading of the name (literal smell + metaphorical steed-strength) is universal in classical commentary.]

== Classical Chinese Medicine (中醫) View

_No classical Chinese entry. The drug entered Chinese phytomedicine only in the modern period, principally as an imported supplement/functional-food adopted from Western marketing of Ayurvedic herbs. Any placement on the canonical Chinese axes is reasoned, not citation._

By reasoned placement on the standard Chinese axes — anchored on the Ayurvedic data and the modern pharmacology — ashwagandha would be expected to be sweet (甘) and slightly bitter (微苦), warm (溫) in property (the unusual warming rasāyana feature carries across), entering Kidney (腎), Heart (心), Liver (肝). Actions in TCM vocabulary would map to 補腎助陽 (tonifies Kidney and supports yang), 安神益智 (calms _shén_ and benefits intellect), and 強筋骨 (strengthens sinews and bones). This is interpretive, not classical attribution, and is offered for cross-system comparison only.#footnote[The reasoned placement follows from the Ayurvedic _vāta-kapha-śāmaka_, _uṣṇa-vīrya_, _vājīkara_, _medhya_, _balya_ profile combined with the modern phytopharmacology of withanolide-driven stress / sleep / fertility actions. It is *not* a citation of any classical Chinese source — explicitly flagged.]

== Ayurvedic Dravyaguna (द्रव्यगुण)

Aśvagandhā is one of the great rasāyana drugs and is included in Caraka's foundational lists for rejuvenation, virilification, and Vāta-pacification.#footnote[Aśvagandhā in Caraka Saṃhitā, Cikitsāsthāna 1 (_rasāyana-pāda_) and Cikitsāsthāna 2 (_vājīkaraṇa-pāda_); standard treatment in Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Guḍūcyādi-varga_ (Chunekar & Pandey ed., Chaukhamba); Dhanvantari Nighaṇṭu, Rāja Nighaṇṭu. Translations: Sharma & Dash, _Caraka Saṃhitā_ (Chowkhamba).]

*Rasa (taste):* Tikta (तिक्त, bitter), Kaṣāya (कषाय, astringent), Madhura (मधुर, sweet) — the three together; the kaṣāya-tikta pair dominates the freshly chewed root, with madhura as the deeper, slower note.

*Guṇa (qualities):* Laghu (लघु, light), Snigdha (स्निग्ध, unctuous). Compact descriptor: _laghu-snigdha_ — an unusual classical combination (most heavy-tissue rasāyana drugs are _guru-snigdha_); the lightness explains aśvagandhā's suitability in convalescence from acute Vāta-aggravating illness, where _guru_-rasāyanas would be too cloying.

*Vīrya (potency):* *Uṣṇa* (उष्ण, warming) — *the standout feature*. Most Ayurvedic rasāyanas (milk, śatāvarī, yaṣṭimadhu, āmalakī) are cooling; aśvagandhā is warming. This is what makes it the canonical *Vāta-Kapha* rasāyana (rather than a Pitta-pacifying one) and why it is the rejuvenative of choice for cold-depleted constitutions and chronic Vāta-disorders.

*Vipāka (post-digestive taste):* Madhura (मधुर, sweet).#footnote[Rasa–guṇa–vīrya–vipāka of aśvagandhā: Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Guḍūcyādi-varga_; Dhanvantari Nighaṇṭu. The _laghu-snigdha_ and uṣṇa-vīrya combination is a defining feature and is repeatedly highlighted in modern dravyaguna commentary as the cross-system distinguishing axis from the cooling rasāyanas — cf. P. V. Sharma, _Dravyaguṇa-vijñāna_, vol. II, _aśvagandhā_.]

*Prabhāva (specific action):* Classical commentary repeatedly highlights aśvagandhā's *combined medhya + vājīkara + balya + nidrā-janana* action — the specific co-occurrence of intellect-promotion, virilification, strengthening, and sleep-promotion in one drug — as a *prabhāva-level* feature, not fully predictable from the rasa-guṇa-vīrya-vipāka profile. The double-action of being *simultaneously* a stress-/anxiety-pacifying nidrā-janana *and* an anabolic-virilifying balya/vājīkara is the specific signature; modern pharmacology corroborates this as a real and unusual co-profile. Flagged as commentarial/interpretive _prabhāva_ — the saṃhitās list the actions; commentators frame their co-occurrence as prabhāva.#footnote[On prabhāva as a category: Caraka Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 26.67–72. The _medhya + vājīkara + nidrā-janana_ co-occurrence at prabhāva level is the standard commentarial reading; see P. V. Sharma, _Dravyaguṇa-vijñāna_, vol. II. Explicitly flagged as commentarial/interpretive.]

*Doṣa effect:* Pacifies Vāta and Kapha (_vāta-kapha-śāmaka_); aggravates Pitta in excess or with unsuitable preparation — particularly relevant for warm-blooded / Pitta-excess constitutions and in active inflammatory states.#footnote[Doṣa attribution: Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Guḍūcyādi-varga_; standard dravyaguna. The Pitta-aggravating note is consistent with the _uṣṇa-vīrya_ and is repeatedly flagged in commentary as a real clinical caution.]

*Karma (actions):*
- _Rasāyana_ — rejuvenative, with specific affinity for muscle and nervous tissue.
- _Vājīkaraṇa_, _vṛṣya_, _śukrala_ — virilifying, promotes _śukra_ (sperm) and reproductive vigor; classical male tonic (parallel and complement to śatāvarī's female register).
- _Balya_, _bṛṃhaṇa_ — strengthening, anabolic; classical use for _kṣaya_, _śoṣa_ (wasting), convalescence, post-illness debility.
- _Medhya_ — promotes intellect / memory.
- _Nidrā-janana_ — sleep-promoting; for _anidrā_ (insomnia) with anxiety or restlessness; the *somnifera* epithet captures this.
- _Vātavyādhi-hara_ — relieves Vāta disorders; principal for chronic Vāta-disease (degenerative, neurological, musculoskeletal).
- _Indriya-balya_ — strengthens sense organs.
- _Jvara-ghna_ (chronic vāta-/kapha-jvara) — relieves chronic fevers of vacuity type.
- Used in: _kṣaya_ / _rājayakṣma_ (wasting, _tuberculosis-equivalent_), _vātavyādhi_, _klaibya_ (impotence), _bandhyatva_ (infertility), _anidrā_, _smṛti-hāni_ (memory loss), debility, geriatric weakness, post-partum debility (in modest doses, with milk).
- *Modern Ayurvedic / commentarial use:* anxiety, "stress," chronic fatigue — these framings are modern restatements of classical _vātavyādhi_ / _kṣaya_ / _anidrā_, are well-supported by modern pharmacology, but the specific "anxiety/stress" vocabulary is modern.#footnote[Karma compiled from Caraka Saṃhitā, Cikitsāsthāna 1 (rasāyana), 2 (vājīkaraṇa), 11 (_rājayakṣma_), 28 (_vātavyādhi_); Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Guḍūcyādi-varga_. The modern "stress/anxiety" framing is flagged as modern restatement of classical categories.]

=== Viruddha (विरुद्ध — incompatibilities)

_(tagged by source tier — S = core saṃhitā, N = later nighaṇṭu, F = folk/regional, M = modern/pharmacological)_

- *(S/N) Conditional cautions:* contraindicated in Pitta excess, active _āma_ (undigested food / metabolic toxicity), high _agni_ states, and acute inflammatory fever — the rasāyana-vājīkara warming use is for the depleted-cold-constitution patient, not the hot/inflamed one. _Avasthā_ and _agni_ viruddha apply.
- *(N) Few named saṃyoga viruddhas* — aśvagandhā is more often a vehicle (with milk, ghee, honey, sugar) than a substance with named antagonists.
- *(M) Modern / pharmacological:*
  - *Hyperthyroidism:* aśvagandhā can mildly raise T4 (and to a lesser extent T3); contraindicated in hyperthyroidism; caution in subclinical hyperthyroidism. Concurrent thyroid medication should be monitored.
  - *Pregnancy:* despite classical use in some fertility contexts, the modern formal safety data in pregnancy is *limited*; some texts and modern Ayurvedic clinicians use cautiously, others avoid in the first trimester. Withaferin A has shown teratogenic effects in some animal models. *Use cautiously / avoid in pregnancy* is the conservative modern position.
  - *Sedative / anxiolytic interaction:* additive sedation with benzodiazepines, alcohol, and other CNS depressants.
  - *Immunosuppressants:* potential interaction via immunomodulatory effects.
  - *Hepatotoxicity (rare):* a small number of recent case reports of cholestatic hepatitis with commercial ashwagandha extracts (notably Icelandic cases, 2019–) — _rare_ but documented; suggests caution with prolonged high-dose standardized extracts, and supports clinical monitoring on long courses.
  - *Autoimmune disease (theoretical):* immunostimulant effect raises a theoretical concern in active autoimmune disease (RA, SLE, MS, autoimmune thyroiditis); clinical evidence sparse, but the conservative position avoids in active flares.#footnote[Thyroid effect of ashwagandha: small RCTs and case literature — modest T4-increasing effect reported; verify against current systematic reviews. Pregnancy: traditional vs. modern safety data divergence; conservative modern position is to avoid in pregnancy, especially first trimester — flagged. Hepatotoxicity: case reports in _Liver International_ and the Icelandic cluster (2019–2021) — rare but real; sourcing/extract-quality issues may be contributors. Sedative and immunosuppressant interactions: standard herb–drug-interaction references. All (M) cautions are explicitly flagged as modern, not classical.]

== Traditional Arabic & Islamic Medicine (Ṭibb al-ʿArabī wa-l-Islāmī / Unani)

_Ashwagandha entered Tibb via medieval Indo-Persian transmission and is canonical in Unani under the name_ asgand _— the direct Hindi-Urdu loanword preserved in Arabic-script Unani literature. See_ tibb-al-arabi.typ.

*Arabic / Persian / Urdu:* اسگند (_asgand_) — the canonical Unani name; اسگندھ ناگوری (_asgandh nāgōrī_, "Nagore asgand," after the Rajasthani town of Nagore, the premium production region) — the standard market term. Avicenna (Ibn Sīnā) does not treat it under a distinct Arabic name; the substance enters Tibb via the later Persian-Indian Unani tradition. The plant is recorded in the *Ḥadīqat al-Afkār* and the later Mughal Unani literature.

*Mizāj:* *Hot and Dry in the 2nd–3rd degree* (Mughal Unani sources; the Avicennan Hot-and-Dry classification of similar rasāyana drugs applies). Converges with the Ayurvedic uṣṇa vīrya — clean convergence.

*Af'āl:* *Muqawwī al-bāh* (aphrodisiac — the principal South Asian Unani indication, paralleling the Ayurvedic _vājīkara_); *muqawwī al-aʿṣāb* (nerve tonic, brain tonic); *muqawwī al-jasad* (body-strengthening); *muqawwī al-rūḥ al-ḥaywānī*; *muḥarrik al-bāh*; *muqawwī al-rūḥ al-nafsānī* (psychic-spirit tonic, the modern anxiolytic register); *muḥsin al-nawm* (sleep-promoting — the _somnifera_ epithet's classical correlate, preserved in Unani).

*Istemālāt:* impotence; sexual debility; nervous exhaustion; insomnia of debility pattern; postpartum recovery; chronic Vāta-equivalent (Tibb _amrāḍ al-rīḥ_) joint pain; chronic depression; convalescence.

*Mawāne':* bilious / hot-pattern; pregnancy at therapeutic dose; thyroid disease (modern); active autoimmune (theoretical, modern).

*Muṣliḥ:* milk + sugar / honey + ghee (the canonical pairing, identical to the Ayurvedic register).

*Badal:* satavar (*shaqāqul mishrī*, see shatavari.typ); kāwānch (Mucuna).

*Tibb formulas:* *Maʿjūn-i Asgand* — ashwagandha electuary, the foundational Mughal Unani aphrodisiac compound; *Khamīrah-i Asgand*; *Sufūf-i Asgand*; *Rowghan-i Asgand* (medicated oil); the compound *Maʿjūn-i Salab* (with salep) uses asgand. In modern Indian Unani practice (CCRUM publications), asgand is widely prescribed in compound and as single drug.

*Source attestation:* not in Avicenna or Ibn al-Bayṭār (the substance is post-classical to the medieval Arabic period); fully treated in *Khazāyin al-Adwiya*, vol. III, _asgand_ entry; *Bayāz-e Kabīr*; CCRUM Unani Pharmacopoeia of India.#footnote[Tibb asgand: post-classical to the medieval Arabic Tibb; canonical in South-Asian Unani from the Mughal period onward.]

== Classical Formulas & Preparations

=== Chinese medicine (中醫)

No classical Chinese formulas use aśvagandhā — see above. Modern Chinese herbal-medicine practice has adopted it as a single-herb supplement/extract under modern integrative-medicine framing; this is not a classical Chinese tradition.

=== Ayurveda

- *Aśvagandhā cūrṇa (अश्वगन्धाचूर्ण)* — the simple powdered root, typically 3–6 g taken with warm milk, ghee, or honey; the foundational classical preparation.
- *Aśvagandhāriṣṭa (अश्वगन्धारिष्ट)* — the principal classical fermented preparation; an _ariṣṭa_ (fermented decoction) made from aśvagandhā with jaggery and other supporting herbs (musta, mañjiṣṭhā, haridrā, etc.); _Bhaiṣajyaratnāvalī_ recipe. Used in _vātavyādhi_, _kṣaya_, _unmāda_ (mental disturbance), _anidrā_, debility.
- *Aśvagandhā ghṛta (अश्वगन्धाघृत)* — medicated ghee; for chronic Vāta disorders, infertility, debility, and as a _medhya_ tonic.
- *Aśvagandhādi cūrṇa / leha* — combinations of aśvagandhā with vidārī, śatāvarī, and milk-sugar; classical male / convalescent tonic.
- *Bālā-aśvagandhādi taila* — medicated oil for external massage in Vāta disorders, particularly chronic neuromuscular and joint disease.
- *Cyavanaprāśa (च्यवनप्राश)* — the archetypal _rasāyana_ leha; aśvagandhā is a standard supporting ingredient in the classical recipe.
- *Compound formulas:* aśvagandhā figures in numerous classical compounds — *Lakshādi guggulu* (for bone weakness), *Yogarāj guggulu* (for joint disorders), and various _vātavyādhi_ formulations.#footnote[Formula sources: _Bhaiṣajyaratnāvalī_ (Aśvagandhāriṣṭa, Aśvagandhādi cūrṇa); _Sharangadhara Saṃhitā_ (preparation methods for _ariṣṭa_, _ghṛta_, _taila_); _Ayurvedic Formulary of India_, Part I (standard recipes); _Caraka Saṃhitā_ Cikitsāsthāna 1, 2, 28 (compound contexts). Translations: Murthy, _Sharangadhara Saṃhitā_; Sharma & Dash, _Caraka_.]

=== Western / Greco-Roman and later European medicine

No Greco-Roman pharmacy of _Withania somnifera_ — the species was unknown to Dioscorides, Galen, and Pliny. _Withania_ as a Western drug entered European pharmacy only in the late 19th–20th centuries as a colonial-era Indian import; the "adaptogen" framing of the late 20th century (rooted in Soviet adaptogen research and Western herbalist adoption) is the dominant modern Western tradition. *Modern Western use of ashwagandha is honestly an import from Ayurveda* and should not be dressed in classical Greek-Roman authority.#footnote[The absence of _Withania somnifera_ from Greco-Roman pharmacy is botanical/historical fact; the species is South Asian / African and was unknown to the classical Mediterranean world. The adaptogen framing originates with Soviet pharmacology (Lazarev, Brekhman) in the 1950s–60s and was applied to ashwagandha in Western herbalism in the late 20th century — this is modern Western tradition built on Ayurvedic substrate, flagged as such.]

== Modern Nutrition & Pharmacology

Ashwagandha root is a medicinal, not a food nutrient source. The relevant figures are phytochemical and pharmacological.

*Key constituents:*
- *Withanolides* — the principal active class; C28 steroidal lactones. Major members: *withaferin A* (the most-studied, with anti-cancer and immunomodulatory interest), *withanolide A*, *withanolide D*, *withanoside IV / VI*. Standardized extracts (KSM-66, Sensoril) are characterized by total withanolide content (typically 1.5–5%).
- *Alkaloids:* withanine, somniferine, anferine, tropane-class alkaloids in trace amounts.
- *Steroidal saponins / sitoindosides* — sitoindosides VII–X.
- *Polysaccharides* — immunomodulatory fraction.
- *Iron* — meaningful in the root; supports the classical _rakta-vardhana_ (blood-building) attribution.#footnote[Phytochemistry of _Withania somnifera_: WHO _Monographs on Selected Medicinal Plants_, vol. 4, _Radix Withaniae_; reviews in _Journal of Ethnopharmacology_, _Phytomedicine_, _Journal of Natural Products_. The KSM-66 (Ixoreal) and Sensoril (Natreon) commercial extracts are the dominant clinical-trial materials and are characterized by distinct withanolide profiles; verify against current product literature.]

*Pharmacology of note:*
- *HPA-axis modulation / cortisol-lowering:* multiple RCTs report modest reductions in salivary and serum cortisol with standardized extracts (KSM-66, Sensoril) in stressed adults; the closest modern correlate of the classical _vātavyādhi_ / _anidrā_ rasāyana axis.
- *Anxiolytic effect:* small-to-moderate effects on anxiety scales (HAM-A, PSS) in multiple RCTs; effect sizes are modest but consistent; mechanism plausibly GABAergic / mild benzodiazepine-receptor activity at withanolides.
- *Sleep-promoting:* moderate evidence for improved sleep onset and quality, both in insomnia and in healthy subjects; aligns with the _nidrā-janana_ classical action and the _somnifera_ epithet.
- *Strength / muscle:* RCTs in resistance-training men report modest improvements in muscle mass and strength; modest effect on testosterone in some studies, inconsistent in others; aligns with the classical _vājīkara_ / _balya_ action.
- *Fertility:* small RCTs report improvements in semen parameters (count, motility, morphology) in oligospermic men; classical _śukrala_ / _vājīkara_ corroborated at the clinical level (modestly).
- *Thyroid:* mild T4-raising effect (and slight T3) in subclinical hypothyroidism; *contraindicated in hyperthyroidism* and to be monitored in concurrent thyroid replacement.
- *Withaferin A:* potent NF-κB inhibitor; broad anti-inflammatory and anti-cancer activity in preclinical models; not yet a clinical drug; substantial research interest.
- *Immunomodulatory:* mixed Th1 / Th2 modulation; raises theoretical autoimmune-flare concern (above).
- *GABAergic / cholinergic:* contributes to the calm-promoting and _medhya_ effects.
- *Cross-system convergence:* the _rasāyana + vājīkara + nidrā-janana_ classical triad maps onto the modern cortisol-lowering + fertility-supportive + sleep-promoting cluster — one of the cleaner classical-modern alignments in the herbal pharmacopoeia.#footnote[Clinical-trial literature: multiple small-to-moderate RCTs of KSM-66 and Sensoril for stress / cortisol / sleep / strength / fertility; systematic reviews in _Journal of Ethnopharmacology_, _Phytomedicine_, _Cureus_, _Sleep Medicine_, and related journals — verify against current systematic reviews, as the literature is active and methodological quality is variable (small N, manufacturer-funded studies common). The withaferin-A / NF-κB / oncology research is preclinical and substantial; clinical translation is preliminary.]

*Safe daily dosage / upper limit:*
- *Ayurvedic Pharmacopoeia of India:* root powder, 3–6 g/day, taken with milk, ghee, or honey.
- *Standardized extracts:* commonly *250–600 mg/day* of root extract standardized to ≥1.5–5% withanolides; clinical-trial ranges run from 300–1200 mg/day for 8–12 weeks; long-term safety beyond ~3 months is not robustly characterized.
- *Risk groups (use cautiously or avoid):* (a) *hyperthyroidism* — avoid; (b) *pregnancy* — conservative modern position is to avoid, especially first trimester (limited safety data; withaferin-A teratogenicity in animal models); (c) *active autoimmune flare* — theoretical avoid; (d) concurrent *sedative* therapy (benzodiazepines, alcohol); (e) concurrent *thyroid medication* (monitor); (f) known prior *hepatotoxic reaction* to standardized extracts (rare but documented); (g) *immunosuppressant* therapy (theoretical interaction).
- *Reference figures, not personalized medical advice;* clinical use, especially in the risk groups, should be coordinated with a clinician familiar with the herb.#footnote[Dose ranges: Ayurvedic Pharmacopoeia of India, Part I, vol. 1, _Aśvagandhā_ monograph; WHO _Monographs_, vol. 4; current clinical-trial literature (KSM-66, Sensoril). Hepatotoxicity case series: Icelandic cluster (2019–2021) and others — rare but flagged. These are reference thresholds, not personalized medical advice.]

